From: mratan <mattdratan@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] watchdog: mtk_wdt: do not inherit the bootloader's interrupt mode

When the bootloader leaves the watchdog running, mtk_wdt_init() adopts it:
it sets WDOG_HW_RUNNING and reprograms the timeout, but leaves WDT_MODE
exactly as the bootloader wrote it. Bootloaders arm it with
IRQ_EN | DUAL_EN, so expiry raises an interrupt first and only resets
afterwards.

A hung kernel services no interrupts, so the first stage goes nowhere and
the reset never arrives — precisely in the situation the watchdog exists
for. Measured on an MT6797 board: a deliberate panic, with the watchdog
armed and being petted right up to the panic, left the machine dead for
490 s before it was power-cycled by hand. An armed watchdog that interrupts
a dead kernel is decoration.

This cannot be corrected later in the lifecycle. Because WDOG_HW_RUNNING is
set, the watchdog core never calls ->start, so mtk_wdt_start()'s own
clearing of these bits never runs — not even when userspace opens
/dev/watchdog. And a userspace fix would do nothing for the case that
matters most, a boot that never reaches userspace at all.

So clear IRQ_EN and DUAL_EN during adoption, while probe is the only thing
running. From there expiry is a straight hardware reset; a caller that
genuinely wants a pretimeout interrupt sets the bits back through
mtk_wdt_set_pretimeout().

Apply the DT mode preferences on this path too. mtk_wdt_start() honours
disable_wdt_extrst and reset_by_toprgu already, but it is never called for
an inherited watchdog, so a board that asks for internal reset in its device
tree was silently not getting it.

Verified on an MT6797 board: with the mode corrected at probe, a deliberate
panic with panic_timeout=0 self-recovered in 2 min 33 s with no human
action, and the fresh boot's dmesg shows the driver finding the
bootloader's value again — which is also the proof that a real reset
occurred rather than a warm continuation.

Signed-off-by: mratan <mattdratan@gmail.com>
---
--- a/drivers/watchdog/mtk_wdt.c
+++ b/drivers/watchdog/mtk_wdt.c
@@ -243,8 +243,52 @@
 	wdt_base = mtk_wdt->wdt_base;
 
 	if (readl(wdt_base + WDT_MODE) & WDT_MODE_EN) {
+		u32 reg, fixed;
+
 		set_bit(WDOG_HW_RUNNING, &wdt_dev->status);
 		mtk_wdt_set_timeout(wdt_dev, wdt_dev->timeout);
+
+		/*
+		 * Adopting a watchdog the bootloader left running also adopts
+		 * the bootloader's WDT_MODE, and bootloaders arm it with
+		 * IRQ_EN | DUAL_EN: expiry raises an interrupt first and only
+		 * resets afterwards.
+		 *
+		 * A hung kernel services no interrupts, so that first stage
+		 * goes nowhere and the reset never arrives — precisely when the
+		 * watchdog was the last line of defence. Measured on an MT6797
+		 * board: a deliberate panic with the watchdog armed and being
+		 * petted left the machine dead for 490 s.
+		 *
+		 * It cannot be corrected later. WDOG_HW_RUNNING is set here, so
+		 * the watchdog core never calls ->start and mtk_wdt_start()'s
+		 * own clearing of these bits never runs, not even when
+		 * userspace opens /dev/watchdog. And a userspace fix does
+		 * nothing for the case that matters most: a boot that never
+		 * reaches userspace.
+		 *
+		 * So clear them at probe. From here, expiry is a straight
+		 * hardware reset; a caller that wants a pretimeout interrupt
+		 * sets the bits back through mtk_wdt_set_pretimeout().
+		 *
+		 * Apply the DT mode preferences here too. mtk_wdt_start()
+		 * honours them already, but it is never called for a watchdog
+		 * inherited from the bootloader.
+		 */
+		reg = readl(wdt_base + WDT_MODE);
+		fixed = reg & ~(WDT_MODE_IRQ_EN | WDT_MODE_DUAL_EN);
+
+		if (mtk_wdt->disable_wdt_extrst)
+			fixed &= ~WDT_MODE_EXRST_EN;
+		if (mtk_wdt->reset_by_toprgu)
+			fixed |= WDT_MODE_CNT_SEL;
+
+		if (fixed != reg) {
+			iowrite32(fixed | WDT_MODE_KEY, wdt_base + WDT_MODE);
+			dev_info(wdt_dev->parent,
+				 "WDT_MODE %#010x -> %#010x (reset is now internal and immediate)\n",
+				 reg, fixed);
+		}
 	}
 }
 
